 1079 #uuid "e88a4196-cc79-5e10-bfb8-147ace0b9d95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133183 #edb/ref 1080 "3.6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133183 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133183 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8a67e27e-44af-5360-8715-296d8aa8f8ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133184 #edb/ref 1080 "6.0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133184 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133184 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c7ed4a85-f7b1-57f4-ad92-83fb9147d902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133185 #edb/ref 1080 "4.9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133185 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133185 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cb8fded3-c72f-5c90-9464-ecad8a0470fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133186 #edb/ref 1080 "5.5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133186 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15d61f03-c16d-58bc-a1a1-29d768ab7f9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133187 #edb/ref 1080 "4.2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133187 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133187 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3e5da85f-e7d2-5690-8235-e4df538b2d56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133660 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7395" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133660 #edb/ref 1071 "A coin is biased so that the probability of it landing on heads is $0.4.$ The coin is tossed $7$ times. What is the mean number of times the coin lands on heads?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133660 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da211af4-8acc-56f9-9b5f-8f2d9b026c70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133660 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin lands on heads. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=7$ tosses\n\n- the probability of landing on heads for each toss is $p=0.4$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(7,0.4).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean of the number of times the coin lands on heads is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(7)(0.4) \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133661 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7396" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133661 #edb/ref 1071 "An archer practices shooting $12$ arrows at a target. The archer has a $50\\%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye. What is the variance in the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f20b6aa-5867-5079-ac47-ba1f3f6486f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133661 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of arrows that hit the bull's eye.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ shots\n\n- the probability of hitting the bull's eye for each arrow is $p=50\\%=0.5$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B(12,0.5).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\n So, the variance of the number of arrows hitting the bull's eye is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(12)(0.5)(1-0.5) \\\\  & =(6)(0.5) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133662 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7397" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133662 #edb/ref 1071 "A fair five-sided die is rolled $8$ times. What is the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133662 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c33c8f5f-6640-5d53-8803-8b4cfaccea6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133662 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times we get an even number.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=8$ rolls of the die\n\n- the probability of getting an even number (i.e., a $2,$ or $4$) on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{2}{5} .\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X\\sim B\\left(8,\\dfrac25\\right).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the standard deviation is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]}=\\sqrt{np(1-p)},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the standard deviation of the number of times we get an even number is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{np(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot (1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.92} \\\\  & =1.386\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Mean of a Binomial Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133845 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "eb06ab09-4bf5-5f87-a6cf-d71c2c9d3296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Variance of a Binomial Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133846 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f9e1f419-8442-5192-a1fa-44b5bedeff56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186134527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133847 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186421990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133847 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Standard Deviation of a Binomial Random Variable" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133847 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133847 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c66a7ba5-1019-56bd-b915-bb725ee4a499" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1045 "Mean and Variance of the Binomial Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134018 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c8768805-6c91-5b41-a3a9-4287350b3afa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84488" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134522 #edb/ref 1071 "A softball team has a $60%$ chance of winning each game in a $12$-game season. What is the mean number of games that the team wins during a season?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7b1810d9-74ee-5cc5-b570-fc2f66f9da2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134522 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins in a given season. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=12$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=60%=0.6$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(12,0.6).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean of the number of games won is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =(12)(0.6) \\\\  & =7.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134523 #edb/ref 1071 "A fair six-sided die is rolled $7$ times. What is the mean of the number of times that the outcome of a single roll is a $2$ or a $3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41a88797-8a0f-5a6f-a8b9-7def35d693e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134523 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times a $2$ or $3$ is rolled.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=7$ rolls\n\n- the probability of getting a $2$ or $3$ on any roll of the die is\n\n$$\np=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(7,\\frac{1}{3}).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the mean is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the mean number of times we get a $2$ or a $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134524 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186133182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134524 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134524 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134524 #edb/ref 1071 "A football team has a $30%$ chance of winning each game in a $20$-game season. What is the variance of the number of games that the football team wins during a season?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b33ecee-6694-5f5b-b468-fb60f3c15a41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134524 #edb/ref 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of games that the team wins in a given season. \n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=20$ games\n\n- the probability of winning each game is $p=30%=0.3$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(20,0.3).$\n\nFor a binomial random variable $X,$ the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSo, the variance of the number of games won is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =(20)(0.3)(1-0.3) \\\\  & =(6)(0.7) \\\\  & =4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134774 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134774 #edb/ref 1061 27404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134774 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0b2b3592-e0b4-5a5e-93ad-e27455156dd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134774 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134775 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134775 #edb/ref 1061 2740